so a key-value store is in essence a  distributed hash table it is a way for  you to store very simple objects blobs  or whatever it cross a cluster of  machines and what the cluster does is it  provides all the maintenance and  failover for you so that you can really  store an enormous number of Records with  extremely low latencies keep key value  stores actually the simplest thing to  describe if you have a piece of data you  give it a name and you say store it it's  actually not that different from how you  would store files on a file system the  advantage of a key value store is it  does this in a massively distributed way  where the nodes themselves can handle  failures and the data is replicated now  what's interesting about key value  stores is that these are systems that  are designed to have to handle truly  massive amounts of traffic  so in our test with the major key value  store vendors like Couchbase and  aerospike these vendors are both able to  handle transaction volumes into the  millions of Records per second  millions of operations per second that's  something they were able to do without  too much trouble now where these  databases tend to blur a bit is both  aerospike and Couchbase the two biggest  key value vendors that we encounter they  have both added document functionality  in recent releases within the past seven  or eight months so the line between  document databases and key value stores  is a bit blurred similarly even though  the secondary index support on these  column stores has also blurred the lines  a bit between document databases and  column families stores making the  distinction between them a little bit  harder to gauge it's more of one of  nuance than have completely different  types and I should say last of all that  document databases and column family  databases do store their data by keys  they are also key  stores and can function perfectly  adequately as a key value store  themselves so the real distinction is  are you more interested in raw scale and  raw power are you more interested in  functionality on the edge cases around  document indexing and so forth or or one  of the use cases alike time series data  that column families have been really  effective at
 

